nodes.statement:
We use $e_i$ for the $i$-th canonical basis vector in $\mathbb{R}^N$, which consists of all zero components,
except for its $i$-th entry which is equal to one,
and use $\nu$ to denote a unit vector in $\mathbb{R}^d$.

Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a first-order algorithm for solving the optimization problem
\begin{align*}
    \begin{aligned}
    (M)\quad \min\{ f(x):\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d\}.
    \end{aligned}

\end{align*}

\begin{aligned}
    \max \quad & f(x_N)-f(x_\ast) \\
    \text{s.t. }\quad
                        & f \in C^{1,1}_L(\mathbb{R}^d) \text{, $f$ is convex},\\
                        & x_{i+1}={\cal A}(x_0,\dots,x_i; f(x_0),\dots,f(x_i); f'(x_0),\dots, f'(x_i)), \; i=0,
                        \ldots, N-1,\\
                        & x_\ast \in X_\ast(f), \|x_\ast-x_0\|\leq R,\\
                        & x_0, \ldots, x_N, x_\ast \in\mathbb{R}^d.
    \end{aligned}
    \tag{P}

